Brute Force
-----------
Idea:
- Store elements in a temporary array.
- Since the array is sorted, keep track of the count of each element.
- Add an element to temp only if its count is at most 2.
- Copy temp back to nums.

Algorithm:
1. Initialize temp with nums[0].
2. Maintain count = 1.
3. Traverse from index 1.
4. If current element equals previous:
   - Increment count.
   - If count <= 2, add it to temp.
5. Otherwise:
   - Reset count = 1.
   - Add the new element.
6. Copy temp back to nums.
7. Return temp.size().

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int removeDuplicates(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        if (n <= 2) return n;

        vector<int> temp;
        temp.push_back(nums[0]);

        int count = 1;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            if (nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) {
                count++;
                if (count <= 2)
                    temp.push_back(nums[i]);
            } else {
                count = 1;
                temp.push_back(nums[i]);
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < temp.size(); i++)
            nums[i] = temp[i];

        return temp.size();
    }
};
```

Better Solution
---------------
Idea:
- Use two pointers.
- The first two elements are always valid.
- For every new element, compare it with the element two positions before the current write index.
- If they are different, keep the current element.

Algorithm:
1. If n <= 2, return n.
2. Initialize k = 2.
3. Traverse i from 2 to n-1.
4. If nums[i] != nums[k-2]:
   - nums[k] = nums[i]
   - k++
5. Return k.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int removeDuplicates(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        if (n <= 2) return n;

        int k = 2;

        for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
            if (nums[i] != nums[k - 2]) {
                nums[k++] = nums[i];
            }
        }

        return k;
    }
};
```

Optimal Solution
----------------
No intermediate improvement exists. The above two-pointer approach is already the optimal solution.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

Interview Notes
---------------
- Since the array is sorted, duplicates appear consecutively.
- The trick is comparing the current element with nums[k-2] instead of nums[k-1].
- If nums[i] == nums[k-2], then adding it would create three consecutive duplicates.
- The first two elements are always valid because each value may appear at most twice.
- Edge Cases:
  - Empty array.
  - One or two elements.
  - All elements identical.
  - No duplicates.
- Follow-up:
  - How would you modify the solution if each element could appear at most K times?
    - Initialize k = K and replace nums[k-2] with nums[k-K].
- This "compare with k-th previous element" pattern is a very common interview variation.
